WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:02.400
Have you ever seen these ads online?

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:08.500
There’s a guy (always seems to be a guy)
talking to the camera about how successful he is.

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:13.611
The production value is low, but there’s
always some impressive swag on display, like

00:00:13.611 --> 00:00:19.670
a Lambo chillin in the garage or an 8 bedroom
mansion with an infinity pool.

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:24.449
If you stick with the video, you’ll probably
hear an inspiring rags-to-riches story with

00:00:24.449 --> 00:00:27.610
a hint at some surefire secret to his success.

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:32.780
Now, I’m a licensed financial professional
who knows there’s no magic pill to becoming

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:37.890
wealthy...but even I found myself wondering:
Are these guys for real?

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:42.010
Is this just slick marketing for boilerplate
financial advice?

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:46.100
Or is this the old song and dance... of a
con-man?

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:57.940
The “Con” in con-man is short for confidence.

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:03.640
In 1849 The New York Herald ran a story about
a well-dressed gentleman by the name of

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:05.120
William Thompson.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:09.431
He would approach a perfect stranger and ask
if they had “confidence in him” to trust

00:01:09.431 --> 00:01:11.750
their watch to him for the day.

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:17.390
The novelty of the request combined with Williams
outward appearance of trustworthiness convinced

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:18.710
dozens to play along.

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:23.479
That was the last most of them ever saw of
their watch or Mr. Thompson.

00:01:23.479 --> 00:01:28.630
We may think of a “con-man” as being the
same thing as a thief or liar.

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:33.590
But in her book “The Confidence Game”,
Maria Konnikova explains “The true con doesn’t

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:35.100
force us to do anything.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:36.439
He doesn’t steal.

00:01:36.439 --> 00:01:37.600
We give.”

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:39.680
In short, they’re master manipulators.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:42.410
And our culture is obsessed with them.

00:01:42.410 --> 00:01:48.020
We make movies, podcasts and documentaries
about their schemes, like Fyre festival’s

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:52.920
Billy McFarland, Elizabeth Holmes of Theranos,
and of course Bernie Madoff.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:58.979
Konnikova explains that.”Transition is the
con’s greatest ally because transition breeds

00:01:58.979 --> 00:02:00.070
uncertainty.”

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:04.340
I guess Littlefinger was right, Chaos really
is a ladder!

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:10.329
And in our era of rapid technological innovation
and shifting online social norms, the conman’s

00:02:10.329 --> 00:02:15.189
hunting ground is more fertile than ever and
we are ripe for the taking.

00:02:15.189 --> 00:02:21.110
Thanks to intensely detailed consumer monitoring
(check out our video about companies stalking

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:26.920
you online) it’s become easier than ever
to pinpoint an audience interested in a certain

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:31.040
topic--for instance, someone who might be
curious about investing because they watch

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:34.180
a YouTube show about financial literacy.

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:40.480
Topics like money and real estate are especially
promising because they’re not widely understood.

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:45.919
And that lack of knowledge--believe it or
not--actually makes people LESS cautious.

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:47.520
This is because of something called the

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:49.360
Dunning-Kreuger effect.

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:53.920
Social psychologists David Dunning and Justin
Kruger conducted multiple studies where people

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:59.540
were tested on subjects like logic &amp; grammar
then asked to rank themselves on how well

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:01.040
they thought they did.

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:05.989
The participants who scored the lowest tended
to rank themselves the highest, and the students

00:03:05.989 --> 00:03:09.799
with high competence tended to underestimate
their performance.

00:03:09.799 --> 00:03:14.140
Basically, the less knowledge you have, the
more you’ll overestimate your abilities.

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:18.219
So if you don’t know anything about investing,
you’re much more likely to believe someone

00:03:18.219 --> 00:03:20.209
who tells you you can beat the markets.

00:03:20.209 --> 00:03:25.709
That’s not to say there aren’t any honest-to-goodness
golden opportunities out there, but sadly

00:03:25.709 --> 00:03:28.239
they are vastly outnumbered.

00:03:28.239 --> 00:03:30.459
So how can you tell the difference?

00:03:30.459 --> 00:03:34.269
Well, most cons tend to follow a familiar
script.

00:03:34.269 --> 00:03:39.819
First, the con identifies their potential
victim, known as the mark.

00:03:39.819 --> 00:03:44.269
Your browser history and tracking cookies
on your computer and phone make this very

00:03:44.269 --> 00:03:45.499
very easy.

00:03:45.499 --> 00:03:49.120
The next step is to befriend them and earn
their trust.

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:52.049
That’s where the wealthy selfie comes in.

00:03:52.049 --> 00:03:55.010
This conveys success, but also approachability.

00:03:55.010 --> 00:04:00.989
He’s not a slick marketer--just an average
bro, like me!

00:04:00.989 --> 00:04:06.799
Next they drop a hint about the reason behind
their success, usually some secret, easy-to-learn

00:04:06.799 --> 00:04:10.469
process that doesn’t require any intense
training or hard work.

00:04:10.469 --> 00:04:12.879
And they’re willing to share it with you!

00:04:12.879 --> 00:04:17.690
Then they provide social proof of their authenticity,
like hyperbolic testimonials or numbers of

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:20.150
followers on Twitter and Instagram.

00:04:20.150 --> 00:04:22.019
Money finally changes hands.

00:04:22.019 --> 00:04:27.830
This might take the form of a special introductory
offer or easy minimum commitment.

00:04:27.830 --> 00:04:32.620
Which the mark is usually all too eager to
take part in, thinking that big profits are

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:34.439
just on the horizon.

00:04:34.439 --> 00:04:36.830
Finally, we reach the sad conclusion.

00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:40.940
The money’s gone and the hoped-for profits
never materialized.

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:46.780
If executed by a master, the mark won’t
even complain, blaming his own bad luck or

00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:50.760
lack of faith rather than the guy who took
him for a ride.

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:55.540
In this day and age, it’s not likely that
you hand over your money and get absolutely

00:04:55.550 --> 00:04:59.030
nothing, unless you’re trying to help out
a Nigerian prince.

00:04:59.030 --> 00:05:06.460
But often the actual product or program you
get is sub-par, hard to execute or just boilerplate

00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:10.500
pump-you-up self help advice designed to boost
your confidence.

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:15.419
All while maintaining the impression that
the pot of gold is just around the corner,

00:05:15.419 --> 00:05:20.400
to keep you moving through their highly curated
sales funnel into more and more expensive

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:21.400
offerings.

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:24.180
Now, is that a con?

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:26.009
Depends on who you ask!

00:05:26.009 --> 00:05:32.001
But consider this, how much time, money, and
energy are going into the sales pitch, as

00:05:32.001 --> 00:05:35.270
compared to the product or service itself?

00:05:35.270 --> 00:05:39.870
Just because the video looks cheap doesn’t
mean they didn’t spend gobs of money plastering

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:41.300
it everywhere.

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:47.219
And does buying the initial product seem to
open you up to increasingly expensive programs?

00:05:47.219 --> 00:05:52.240
Those are all major red flags that you’re
being sold an empty promise.

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:56.330
If you’re realizing you’ve fallen for
something like this in the past, don’t feel

00:05:56.330 --> 00:05:57.330
bad.

00:05:57.330 --> 00:06:01.580
The fact that we’re all a little gullible
is actually a positive thing.

00:06:01.580 --> 00:06:07.260
As Konnikova explains “The simple truth
is that most people aren’t out to get you...Trust...

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:12.460
not adeptness at spotting deception, is the
more evolutionary beneficial path.”

00:06:12.469 --> 00:06:17.110
And while modern technology might be making
new opportunities for con-artists, it’s

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:19.990
also never been easier for you to uncover
them.

00:06:19.990 --> 00:06:26.040
So do your research and ask yourself, if they
have all this money, why do they need yours?

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:27.800
And that’s our two cents!

